biguous_name","senseReview":"Reviewed complete original name headword and source subject. Article covers the biblical person(s), named place, or named spiritual figure denoted by this exact spelling; multiple persons under one unchanged source article remain together. Actual proper-name capitalization and whole-name boundaries required. Explicit occurrence-level ambiguity exclusions override this rule.","sourceHeadwordId":"Ahab","term":"Ahab"}],"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","matchingRules":["reviewed_exact_headword"],"sourceProvenanceId":"Ahab","sourceTitle":"Ahab"},{"evidence":[{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"Elijah","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_unambiguous_name","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Elijah::Elijah::reviewed_unambiguous_name","sourceHeadwordId":"Elijah"},"text":"But the angel of the LORD said to Elijah the Tishbite, “Go up to meet the messengers of the king of Samaria and ask them, ‘Is it because there is no God in Israel that you are on your way to inquire of Baal-zebub, the god of Ekron?’","verse":3},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"Elijah","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_unambiguous_name","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Elijah::Elijah::reviewed_unambiguous_name","sourceHeadwordId":"Elijah"},"text":"Therefore this is what the LORD says: ‘You will not get up from the bed on which you are lying. You will surely die.’” So Elijah departed.","verse":4},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"Elijah","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_unambiguous_name","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Elijah::Elijah::reviewed_unambiguous_name","sourceHeadwordId":"Elijah"},"text":"“He was a hairy man,” they answered, “with a leather belt around his waist.” “It was Elijah the Tishbite,” said the king.","verse":8},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"Elijah","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_unambiguous_name","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Elijah::Elijah::reviewed_unambiguous_name","sourceHeadwordId":"Elijah"},"text":"Then King Ahaziah sent to Elijah a captain with his company of fifty men. So the captain went up to Elijah, who was sitting on top of a hill, and said to him, “Man of God, the king declares, ‘Come down!’”","verse":9},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"Elijah","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_unambiguous_name","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Elijah::Elijah::reviewed_unambiguous_name","sourceHeadwordId":"Elijah"},"text":"Elijah answered the captain, “If I am a man of God, may fire come down from heaven and consume you and your fifty men.” And fire came down from heaven and consumed the captain and his fifty men.","verse":10},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"Elijah","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_unambiguous_name","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Elijah::Elijah::reviewed_unambiguous_name","sourceHeadwordId":"Elijah"},"text":"So the king sent to Elijah another captain with his fifty men. And the captain said to Elijah, “Man of God, the king declares, ‘Come down at once!’”","verse":11},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"Elijah","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_unambiguous_name","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Elijah::Elijah::reviewed_unambiguous_name","sourceHeadwordId":"Elijah"},"text":"Again Elijah replied, “If I am a man of God, may fire come down from heaven and consume you and your fifty men.” And the fire of God came down from heaven and consumed the captain and his fifty men.","verse":12},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"Elijah","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_unambiguous_name","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Elijah::Elijah::reviewed_unambiguous_name","sourceHeadwordId":"Elijah"},"text":"So the king sent a third captain with his fifty men. And the third captain went up, fell on his knees before Elijah, and begged him, “Man of God, may my life and the lives of these fifty servants of yours please be precious in your sight.","verse":13},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"Elijah","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_unambiguous_name","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Elijah::Elijah::reviewed_unambiguous_name","sourceHeadwordId":"Elijah"},"text":"Then the angel of the LORD said to Elijah, “Go down with him. Do not be afraid of him.” So Elijah got up and went down with him to the king.","verse":15},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"Elijah","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_unambiguous_name","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Elijah::Elijah::reviewed_unambiguous_name","sourceHeadwordId":"Elijah"},"text":"And Elijah said to King Ahaziah, “This is what the LORD says: Is there really no God in Israel for you to inquire of His word? Is that why you have sent messengers to inquire of Baal-zebub, the god of Ekron? Therefore you will not get up from the bed on which you are lying. You will surely die.”","verse":16},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"Elijah","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_unambiguous_name","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Elijah::Elijah::reviewed_unambiguous_name","sourceHeadwordId":"Elijah"},"text":"So Ahaziah died according to the word of the LORD that Elijah had spoken. And since he had no son, Jehoram succeeded him in the second year of the reign of Jehoram son of Jehoshaphat over Judah.","verse":17}],"id":"Elijah","label":"Elijah","matchReviews":[{"ruleId":"Elijah::Elijah::reviewed_unambiguous_name","scope":"reviewed_unambiguous_name","senseReview":"Reviewed complete original name headword and source subject. Article covers the biblical person(s), named place, or named spiritual figure denoted by this exact spelling; multiple persons under one unchanged source article remain together. Actual proper-name capitalization and whole-name boundaries required. Explicit occurrence-level ambiguity exclusions override this rule.","sourceHeadwordId":"Elijah","term":"Elijah"}],"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","matchingRules":["reviewed_exact_headword"],"sourceProvenanceId":"Elijah","sourceTitle":"Elijah"},{"evidence":[{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"But the angel of the LORD said to Elijah the Tishbite, “Go up to meet the messengers of the king of Samaria and ask them, ‘Is it because there is no God in Israel that you are on your way to inquire of Baal-zebub, the god of Ekron?’","verse":3},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"Therefore this is what the LORD says: ‘You will not get up from the bed on which you are lying. You will surely die.’” So Elijah departed.","verse":4},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"They replied, “A man came up to meet us and said, ‘Go back to the king who sent you and tell him that this is what the LORD says: Is it because there is no God in Israel that you are sending these men to inquire of Baal-zebub, the god of Ekron? Therefore you will not get up from the bed on which you are lying. You will surely die.’”","verse":6},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"Then the angel of the LORD said to Elijah, “Go down with him. Do not be afraid of him.” So Elijah got up and went down with him to the king.","verse":15},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"And Elijah said to King Ahaziah, “This is what the LORD says: Is there really no God in Israel for you to inquire of His word? Is that why you have sent messengers to inquire of Baal-zebub, the god of Ekron? Therefore you will not get up from the bed on which you are lying. You will surely die.”","verse":16},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"So Ahaziah died according to the word of the LORD that Elijah had spoken. And since he had no son, Jehoram succeeded him in the second year of the reign of Jehoram son of Jehoshaphat over Judah.","verse":17}],"id":"Lord","label":"LORD","matchReviews":[{"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","scope":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","senseReview":"Only literal uppercase LORD is admitted globally. Source Lord article explicitly covers God as Lord; lowercase human lord and sentence-title Lord are not inferred from this rule.","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord","term":"LORD"}],"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","matchingRules":["reviewed_exact_headword"],"sourceProvenanceId":"Lord","sourceTitle":"Lord"},{"evidence":[{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"Samaria","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_unambiguous_name","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Samaria::Samaria::reviewed_unambiguous_name","sourceHeadwordId":"Samaria"},"text":"Now Ahaziah had fallen through the lattice of his upper room in Samaria and injured himself. So he sent messengers and instructed them: “Go inquire of Baal-zebub, the god of Ekron, whether I will recover from this injury.”","verse":2},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"Samaria","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_unambiguous_name","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Samaria::Samaria::reviewed_unambiguous_name","sourceHeadwordId":"Samaria"},"text":"But the angel of the LORD said to Elijah the Tishbite, “Go up to meet the messengers of the king of Samaria and ask them, ‘Is it because there is no God in Israel that you are on your way to inquire of Baal-zebub, the god of Ekron?’","verse":3}],"id":"Samaria","label":"Samaria","matchReviews":[{"ruleId":"Samaria::Samaria::reviewed_unambiguous_name","scope":"reviewed_unambiguous_name","senseReview":"Reviewed complete original name headword and source subject. Article covers the biblical person(s), named place, or named spiritual figure denoted by this exact spelling; multiple persons under one unchanged source article remain together. Actual proper-name capitalization and whole-name boundaries required. Explicit occurrence-level ambiguity exclusions override this rule.","sourceHeadwordId":"Samaria","term":"Samaria"}],"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","matchingRules":["reviewed_exact_headword"],"sourceProvenanceId":"Samaria","sourceTitle":"Samaria"},{"evidence":[{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"upper room","senseEvidence":{"basis":"source_verse_citation","originalCitations":[{"anchorIndex":2,"href":"?bref=2Kgs.1.2","rawReference":"2Kgs.1.2"}],"ruleId":"UpperRoom::Upper Room::source_verse_citation","sourceHeadwordId":"UpperRoom"},"text":"Now Ahaziah had fallen through the lattice of his upper room in Samaria and injured himself. So he sent messengers and instructed them: “Go inquire of Baal-zebub, the god of Ekron, whether I will recover from this injury.”","verse":2}],"id":"UpperRoom","label":"upper room","matchReviews":[{"ruleId":"UpperRoom::Upper Room::source_verse_citation","scope":"source_verse_citation","senseReview":"Reviewed full original phrase headword in its stated biblical sense. The entire phrase must occur and this article must explicitly cite the same verse; no shortened fragment or inferred topical synonym is matched.","sourceHeadwordId":"UpperRoom","term":"Upper Room"}],"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","matchingRules":["reviewed_exact_headword"],"sourceProvenanceId":"UpperRoom","sourceTitle":"Upper Room"}],"format":"bsb-study-chapter-v2","notes":[{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=2Kgs.1.1","r":"2Kgs.1.1","rawR":"2Kgs.1.1","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Kgs.1.1","value":"2Kgs.1.1"}],"t":"ref","v":"1:1"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," After ",{"c":["Ahab’s death,"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," the Moabites felt secure enough to declare themselves free of their Israelite overlords (see ",{"href":"?bref=2Kgs.3.4-5","r":"2Kgs.3.4-5","rawR":"2Kgs.3.4-5","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Kgs.3.4-5","value":"2Kgs.3.4-5"}],"t":"ref","v":"3:4-5"},"). The historical notice anticipates the troubles that Israel would experience after the short-lived reign of Ahaziah (see ",{"href":"?bref=1Kgs.22.51","r":"1Kgs.22.51","rawR":"1Kgs.22.51","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Kgs.22.51","value":"1Kgs.22.51"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Kgs 22:51"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:IIKgs.1.1","refs":[{"book":"2KI","endChapter":1,"endVerse":1,"startChapter":1,"startVerse":1}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"IIKgs.1.1","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"2KI","sourceChapterCoverage":[1],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"2a08521bcafac25507e328701d11c241d69d559ca47704824d83c5c923525e44","sourceItemName":"IIKgs.1.1","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":4371,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"2KI","canonicalOSIS":"2Kgs","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"2Kgs"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"2Kgs.1.1","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"IIKgs.1.1","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=2Kgs.1.2","r":"2Kgs.1.2","rawR":"2Kgs.1.2","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Kgs.1.2","value":"2Kgs.1.2"}],"t":"ref","v":"1:2"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," An ",{"c":["upper room"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," or balcony enclosed with ",{"c":["latticework"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," was common; it allowed for the free flow of air and a degree of privacy, but ",{"c":["latticework"],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"}," was easily broken. • ",{"c":["Baal-zebub"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," (",{"c":["lord of flies"],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"},") may be a parody of a Canaanite deity named Baal-zebul (",{"c":["Baal is prince"],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"},"). The name of this deity was later used for Satan (see ",{"href":"?item=Matt.10.25_StudyNote_Filament","id":"Matt.10.25_StudyNote_Filament","rawId":"Matt.10.25_StudyNote_Filament","t":"link","v":"study note on Matt 10:25"},"). Ahaziah followed his father Ahab in worshiping Baal."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:IIKgs.1.2","refs":[{"book":"2KI","endChapter":1,"endVerse":2,"startChapter":1,"startVerse":2}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"IIKgs.1.2","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"2KI","sourceChapterCoverage":[1],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"466653206fd5c925473211b5eb5915e62a0a29f925c9b01d3879a0504375293d","sourceItemName":"IIKgs.1.2","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":4372,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"2KI","canonicalOSIS":"2Kgs","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"2Kgs"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"2Kgs.1.2","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"IIKgs.1.2","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=2Kgs.1.3-4","r":"2Kgs.1.3-4","rawR":"2Kgs.1.3-4","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Kgs.1.3-4","value":"2Kgs.1.3-4"}],"t":"ref","v":"1:3-4"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["The angel of the"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["Lord"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt-divine-name","t":"span"}," does not seem to be a christophany in the book of Kings, as in other Old Testament texts (e.g., ",{"href":"?bref=Judg.6.11-18","r":"Judg.6.11-18","rawR":"Judg.6.11-18","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Judg.6.11-18","value":"Judg.6.11-18"}],"t":"ref","v":"Judg 6:11-18"},"). • ",{"c":["Elijah"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"},", who had previously confronted Ahaziah’s father, Ahab, had a twofold message for the king: a condemnation for failure to acknowledge that Yahweh, not Baal, is God (see ",{"href":"?bref=1Kgs.18.16-18","r":"1Kgs.18.16-18","rawR":"1Kgs.18.16-18","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Kgs.18.16-18","value":"1Kgs.18.16-18"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Kgs 18:16-18"},"), and a pronouncement of doom (see ",{"href":"?bref=1Kgs.21.17-24","r":"1Kgs.21.17-24","rawR":"1Kgs.21.17-24","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Kgs.21.17-24","value":"1Kgs.21.17-24"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Kgs 21:17-24"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:IIKgs.1.3-4","refs":[{"book":"2KI","endChapter":1,"endVerse":4,"startChapter":1,"startVerse":3}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"IIKgs.1.3-4","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"2KI","sourceChapterCoverage":[1],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"f3d9cae1d25a1c295f9ed03547d8874d6ca2927c1ef6d3b25d27159ab33ab3a8","sourceItemName":"IIKgs.1.3-4","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":4373,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"2KI","canonicalOSIS":"2Kgs","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"2Kgs"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"2Kgs.1.3-4","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"IIKgs.1.3-4","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=2Kgs.1.9-10","r":"2Kgs.1.9-10","rawR":"2Kgs.1.9-10","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Kgs.1.9-10","value":"2Kgs.1.9-10"}],"t":"ref","v":"1:9-10"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," The arrogant words of the ",{"c":["army captain"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," contained a contradiction, which ",{"c":["Elijah"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," pointed out: If Elijah was a ",{"c":["man of God"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," (i.e., God’s prophet), then his authority was from God and to ignore him was to invite peril (",{"href":"?bref=Deut.18.15-19","r":"Deut.18.15-19","rawR":"Deut.18.15-19","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.18.15-19","value":"Deut.18.15-19"}],"t":"ref","v":"Deut 18:15-19"},"). • ",{"c":["Fire"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," often symbolizes the presence of God in the Scriptures (see ",{"href":"?bref=Exod.3.1-6","r":"Exod.3.1-6","rawR":"Exod.3.1-6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Exod.3.1-6","value":"Exod.3.1-6"}],"t":"ref","v":"Exod 3:1-6"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Exod.19.18","r":"Exod.19.18","rawR":"Exod.19.18","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Exod.19.18","value":"Exod.19.18"}],"t":"ref","v":"19:18"},"; cp. ",{"href":"?bref=1Kgs.18.38","r":"1Kgs.18.38","rawR":"1Kgs.18.38","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Kgs.18.38","value":"1Kgs.18.38"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Kgs 18:38"},"). • ",{"c":["Fifty soldiers"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," was a common grouping (",{"href":"?bref=1Kgs.18.4","r":"1Kgs.18.4","rawR":"1Kgs.18.4","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Kgs.18.4","value":"1Kgs.18.4"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Kgs 18:4"},"); here it indicates a division within Israel’s military organization."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:IIKgs.1.9-10","refs":[{"book":"2KI","endChapter":1,"endVerse":10,"startChapter":1,"startVerse":9}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"IIKgs.1.9-10","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"2KI","sourceChapterCoverage":[1],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"45993a1626e307a7a25376672e883719915013d55a545827c38f43b8a1088e22","sourceItemName":"IIKgs.1.9-10","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":4374,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"2KI","canonicalOSIS":"2Kgs","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"2Kgs"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"2Kgs.1.9-10","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"IIKgs.1.9-10","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=2Kgs.1.11-13","r":"2Kgs.1.11-13","rawR":"2Kgs.1.11-13","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Kgs.1.11-13","value":"2Kgs.1.11-13"}],"t":"ref","v":"1:11-13"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," Report of what happened to the previous contingent had apparently reached the king."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:IIKgs.1.11-13","refs":[{"book":"2KI","endChapter":1,"endVerse":13,"startChapter":1,"startVerse":11}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"IIKgs.1.11-13","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"2KI","sourceChapterCoverage":[1],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"5330d3a5115e2bc7026fb23ae44bc7e3bf9c175e514212d73863b462fd57b5ca","sourceItemName":"IIKgs.1.11-13","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":4375,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"2KI","canonicalOSIS":"2Kgs","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"2Kgs"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"2Kgs.1.11-13","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"IIKgs.1.11-13","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=2Kgs.1.13-14","r":"2Kgs.1.13-14","rawR":"2Kgs.1.13-14","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Kgs.1.13-14","value":"2Kgs.1.13-14"}],"t":"ref","v":"1:13-14"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," In contrast to the first two officers, the third commander ",{"c":["pleaded"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," for mercy before Elijah and God."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:IIKgs.1.13-14","refs":[{"book":"2KI","endChapter":1,"endVerse":14,"startChapter":1,"startVerse":13}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"IIKgs.1.13-14","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"2KI","sourceChapterCoverage":[1],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"e693d5c9b6f60066f8028ebe6d73d89b7f6a5bf8cbd3c058ec252f1dda321ff6","sourceItemName":"IIKgs.1.13-14","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":4376,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"2KI","canonicalOSIS":"2Kgs","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"2Kgs"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"2Kgs.1.13-14","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"IIKgs.1.13-14","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=2Kgs.1.15","r":"2Kgs.1.15","rawR":"2Kgs.1.15","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Kgs.1.15","value":"2Kgs.1.15"}],"t":"ref","v":"1:15"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," When ",{"c":["the angel of the"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["Lord"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt-divine-name","t":"span"}," (",{"href":"?bref=2Kgs.1.3","r":"2Kgs.1.3","rawR":"2Kgs.1.3","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Kgs.1.3","value":"2Kgs.1.3"}],"t":"ref","v":"1:3"},") instructed ",{"c":["Elijah"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"},", he responded in faithful obedience."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:IIKgs.1.15","refs":[{"book":"2KI","endChapter":1,"endVerse":15,"startChapter":1,"startVerse":15}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"IIKgs.1.15","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"2KI","sourceChapterCoverage":[1],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"16db2f318ea2f62d4982f259225fa5aa62f91505f4196d5b7df3a9843598487f","sourceItemName":"IIKgs.1.15","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":4377,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"2KI","canonicalOSIS":"2Kgs","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"2Kgs"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"2Kgs.1.15","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"IIKgs.1.15","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=2Kgs.1.16","r":"2Kgs.1.16","rawR":"2Kgs.1.16","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Kgs.1.16","value":"2Kgs.1.16"}],"t":"ref","v":"1:16"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["Is there no God in Israel?"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," God’s message to Ahaziah remained unchanged (cp. ",{"href":"?bref=2Kgs.1.3-4","r":"2Kgs.1.3-4","rawR":"2Kgs.1.3-4","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Kgs.1.3-4","value":"2Kgs.1.3-4"}],"t":"ref","v":"1:3-4"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:IIKgs.1.16","refs":[{"book":"2KI","endChapter":1,"endVerse":16,"startChapter":1,"startVerse":16}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"IIKgs.1.16","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"2KI","sourceChapterCoverage":[1],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"ca98c175ac138e3ea9315d01370146fb0651337b229c876cb5e0a9b06e839c3e","sourceItemName":"IIKgs.1.16","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":4378,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"2KI","canonicalOSIS":"2Kgs","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"2Kgs"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"2Kgs.1.16","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"IIKgs.1.16","ty